1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,632
This hand saved your life at the Granicus,

2
00:00:02,632 --> 00:00:03,798
Alexander! This hand!

3
00:00:04,048 --> 00:00:06,143
Sit down, Cleitus. You're drunk.

4
00:00:06,343 --> 00:00:08,660
Your father Philip won your wars for you!

5
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:10,807
And now you dress like a Persian king!

6
00:00:11,057 --> 00:00:13,325
Cleitus, stop! Alexander, he doesn't mean it!

7
00:00:13,525 --> 00:00:15,146
Guards! Give me a spear!

8
00:00:15,346 --> 00:00:16,656
Alexander! No!

9
00:00:17,756 --> 00:00:20,700
Samarkand, three hundred twenty-eight BC.

10
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,500
The most powerful man on Earth has just

11
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:25,870
killed one of his oldest friends.

12
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:28,069
I've known him since we were boys.

13
00:00:28,069 --> 00:00:29,185
We sat side by side,

14
00:00:29,185 --> 00:00:30,414
in the same classroom.

15
00:00:30,714 --> 00:00:33,216
And your teacher was a man the whole

16
00:00:33,216 --> 00:00:36,135
world still knows. His name was Aristotle.

17
00:00:36,135 --> 00:00:38,151
To see how a boy became this,

18
00:00:38,151 --> 00:00:39,888
go back to the beginning.

19
00:00:42,088 --> 00:00:44,212
Pella, the capital of Macedon.

20
00:00:44,212 --> 00:00:46,194
Three hundred forty-four BC.

21
00:00:46,194 --> 00:00:48,459
You're twelve, a nobleman's son,

22
00:00:48,459 --> 00:00:51,078
sent to the palace to serve the king.

23
00:00:51,328 --> 00:00:53,745
Thirteen talents, for a horse that won't let

24
00:00:53,745 --> 00:00:55,447
anyone ride him? Take him away.

25
00:00:55,447 --> 00:00:56,161
He's useless.

26
00:00:56,411 --> 00:00:58,385
Useless? You're losing a great horse,

27
00:00:58,385 --> 00:01:00,894
father, because nobody here knows how to handle

28
00:01:00,894 --> 00:01:01,107
him!

29
00:01:01,357 --> 00:01:02,515
Ha! And you do,

30
00:01:02,515 --> 00:01:02,824
boy?

31
00:01:03,024 --> 00:01:04,308
I do. And if I fail,

32
00:01:04,308 --> 00:01:05,848
I'll pay for him myself.

33
00:01:06,148 --> 00:01:08,078
Alexander, careful! He'll kick you!

34
00:01:08,278 --> 00:01:10,342
Look. He's afraid of his own shadow.

35
00:01:10,342 --> 00:01:11,144
So I turn him,

36
00:01:11,144 --> 00:01:12,004
toward the sun.

37
00:01:12,404 --> 00:01:14,064
Plutarch tells this story.

38
00:01:14,064 --> 00:01:16,809
Alexander turned the horse to face the sun,

39
00:01:16,809 --> 00:01:18,724
so he couldn't see his shadow,

40
00:01:18,724 --> 00:01:20,512
and then jumped on his back.

41
00:01:20,762 --> 00:01:23,290
My son, find yourself a kingdom big enough

42
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:25,096
for you. Macedon is too small.

43
00:01:25,396 --> 00:01:27,691
The horse was called Bucephalus.

44
00:01:27,691 --> 00:01:30,919
Ox-head. He would carry Alexander all the way

45
00:01:30,919 --> 00:01:31,564
to India.

46
00:01:33,764 --> 00:01:36,965
A year later, Philip hires the most brilliant

47
00:01:36,965 --> 00:01:39,242
mind in Greece to teach his son.

48
00:01:39,242 --> 00:01:40,380
Not in the city,

49
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:42,799
but in a quiet place in the hills:

50
00:01:42,799 --> 00:01:44,791
the sanctuary of the nymphs,

51
00:01:44,791 --> 00:01:45,431
at Mieza.

52
00:01:45,681 --> 00:01:47,423
Sit. Here, in the shade.

53
00:01:47,423 --> 00:01:49,527
Today, we talk about courage.

54
00:01:49,777 --> 00:01:51,458
Courage is easy. You charge,

55
00:01:51,458 --> 00:01:52,539
and you never run.

56
00:01:52,789 --> 00:01:55,634
Then a wild boar is the bravest creature

57
00:01:55,634 --> 00:01:58,409
alive. No. Courage lives in the middle,

58
00:01:58,409 --> 00:02:01,752
between running away, and running at death like

59
00:02:01,752 --> 00:02:02,250
a fool.

60
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:04,374
So, a brave man is still a little

61
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:04,982
bit afraid?

62
00:02:05,182 --> 00:02:07,258
Exactly. Too much is a mistake.

63
00:02:07,258 --> 00:02:08,865
Too little is a mistake.

64
00:02:08,865 --> 00:02:10,941
Good things live in the middle.

65
00:02:11,341 --> 00:02:13,298
Aristotle really taught this.

66
00:02:13,298 --> 00:02:15,526
Today we call it the golden mean.

67
00:02:15,526 --> 00:02:18,631
Remember it. Your classmate is going to forget

68
00:02:18,631 --> 00:02:18,833
it.

69
00:02:19,133 --> 00:02:21,366
Alexander! Come on! Race you to the caves!

70
00:02:21,566 --> 00:02:23,563
Hephaestion! You'll lose again!

71
00:02:23,863 --> 00:02:26,607
Hephaestion. Alexander's closest friend,

72
00:02:26,607 --> 00:02:28,323
for the rest of his life.

73
00:02:28,323 --> 00:02:31,273
Ancient writers never list the whole class,

74
00:02:31,273 --> 00:02:33,880
so nobody knows for sure if he studied

75
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:34,223
here.

76
00:02:34,523 --> 00:02:36,571
The lessons covered politics,

77
00:02:36,571 --> 00:02:38,620
medicine, plants and animals,

78
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:39,891
and Homer's poems.

79
00:02:40,141 --> 00:02:41,887
Your copy of the Iliad.

80
00:02:41,887 --> 00:02:43,557
I corrected it myself.

81
00:02:43,557 --> 00:02:45,303
Read it like a soldier,

82
00:02:45,303 --> 00:02:46,518
not like a poet.

83
00:02:46,768 --> 00:02:48,461
I'll keep it under my pillow.

84
00:02:48,461 --> 00:02:49,863
Right next to my dagger.

85
00:02:50,163 --> 00:02:52,232
Plutarch says he did exactly that,

86
00:02:52,232 --> 00:02:53,753
for the rest of his life.

87
00:02:54,053 --> 00:02:56,162
Teacher. If I conquer the whole world,

88
00:02:56,162 --> 00:02:57,494
will you be proud of me?

89
00:02:57,744 --> 00:03:00,410
I'll be proud if you can rule yourself.

90
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:03,008
Conquering the world is the easy part.

91
00:03:03,308 --> 00:03:05,772
You can still visit this school today.

92
00:03:05,772 --> 00:03:07,328
Near the town of Naousa,

93
00:03:07,328 --> 00:03:10,115
in northern Greece, the caves and the stone

94
00:03:10,115 --> 00:03:11,736
walkways are still there.

95
00:03:13,936 --> 00:03:15,785
Three hundred thirty-six BC.

96
00:03:15,785 --> 00:03:19,220
King Philip celebrates his daughter's wedding in the

97
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:20,343
theatre at Aegae.

98
00:03:20,593 --> 00:03:21,820
Let the people see me!

99
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:23,437
No guards. I'm among friends!

100
00:03:23,737 --> 00:03:25,710
Who's that man? He's running at the king!

101
00:03:25,910 --> 00:03:27,962
One of Philip's own bodyguards,

102
00:03:27,962 --> 00:03:29,418
a man named Pausanias,

103
00:03:29,418 --> 00:03:32,066
stabbed him in front of the whole crowd.

104
00:03:32,366 --> 00:03:33,321
The king is dead!

105
00:03:33,321 --> 00:03:34,163
Philip is dead!

106
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:36,030
Alexander. You're the king now.

107
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:36,552
At twenty.

108
00:03:36,802 --> 00:03:39,009
Then everyone who thinks I'm just a boy

109
00:03:39,009 --> 00:03:40,593
is about to learn something.

110
00:03:40,993 --> 00:03:42,686
Soon after, in Corinth,

111
00:03:42,686 --> 00:03:45,703
the young king meets the strangest man in

112
00:03:45,703 --> 00:03:48,721
Greece: Diogenes, who lives in a big clay

113
00:03:48,721 --> 00:03:49,015
jar.

114
00:03:49,265 --> 00:03:51,385
I am Alexander, king of Macedon.

115
00:03:51,385 --> 00:03:53,307
Ask me for anything you want.

116
00:03:53,557 --> 00:03:55,601
Then move a little to the side.

117
00:03:55,601 --> 00:03:57,118
You're blocking my sun.

118
00:03:57,418 --> 00:03:58,325
How dare you!

119
00:03:58,525 --> 00:04:00,266
No. If I were not Alexander,

120
00:04:00,266 --> 00:04:02,007
I would want to be Diogenes.

121
00:04:02,307 --> 00:04:03,786
Did this really happen?

122
00:04:03,786 --> 00:04:05,650
Many ancient writers tell it.

123
00:04:05,650 --> 00:04:08,607
But it's so perfect that some historians think

124
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:10,600
it was polished over the years.

125
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,535
Spring, three hundred thirty-four BC.

126
00:04:15,535 --> 00:04:19,897
Alexander crosses into Asia with about thirty-five thousand

127
00:04:19,897 --> 00:04:23,224
men, against the largest empire in the world.

128
00:04:23,474 --> 00:04:24,162
Watch.

129
00:04:24,512 --> 00:04:26,408
He threw his spear into the beach!

130
00:04:26,408 --> 00:04:26,631
Why?

131
00:04:26,831 --> 00:04:28,392
He's claiming it. All of Asia,

132
00:04:28,392 --> 00:04:29,277
won by the spear.

133
00:04:29,577 --> 00:04:32,768
Diodorus writes that Alexander threw a spear into

134
00:04:32,768 --> 00:04:35,438
the shore of Asia before stepping off his

135
00:04:35,438 --> 00:04:37,848
ship. Today, that beach is in Turkey.

136
00:04:38,748 --> 00:04:39,863
A few weeks later,

137
00:04:39,863 --> 00:04:41,227
at the river Granicus,

138
00:04:41,227 --> 00:04:43,891
the Persians are waiting on the other bank.

139
00:04:44,141 --> 00:04:45,204
The river is deep,

140
00:04:45,204 --> 00:04:47,151
my king, and the banks are steep.

141
00:04:47,151 --> 00:04:48,332
Wait until tomorrow.

142
00:04:48,532 --> 00:04:49,995
I crossed the Hellespont,

143
00:04:49,995 --> 00:04:52,220
Parmenion. I'm not afraid of a stream.

144
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:52,805
Follow me!

145
00:04:53,105 --> 00:04:55,907
Alexander charges straight into the river.

146
00:04:55,907 --> 00:04:58,376
His helmet has two tall white plumes,

147
00:04:58,376 --> 00:04:59,977
so everyone can see him.

148
00:05:00,227 --> 00:05:01,466
He's in front of everyone!

149
00:05:01,466 --> 00:05:02,705
They're all aiming at him!

150
00:05:02,905 --> 00:05:06,231
A Persian noble splits Alexander's helmet with an

151
00:05:06,231 --> 00:05:09,151
axe. Another raises his sword for the final

152
00:05:09,151 --> 00:05:09,490
blow.

153
00:05:09,690 --> 00:05:11,235
Alexander! Behind you!

154
00:05:11,435 --> 00:05:12,205
I've got him!

155
00:05:12,505 --> 00:05:14,308
Cleitus, called the Black,

156
00:05:14,308 --> 00:05:16,180
cuts off the Persian's arm.

157
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:17,982
He saves Alexander's life.

158
00:05:17,982 --> 00:05:20,825
Six years later, Alexander will kill him.

159
00:05:21,725 --> 00:05:24,281
Gordion, in the middle of Anatolia.

160
00:05:24,281 --> 00:05:27,495
An old legend says: whoever unties this knot

161
00:05:27,495 --> 00:05:29,102
will rule all of Asia.

162
00:05:29,352 --> 00:05:30,712
Nobody can find the ends.

163
00:05:30,712 --> 00:05:32,887
People have tried for hundreds of years.

164
00:05:33,087 --> 00:05:35,343
Then it doesn't matter how I untie it.

165
00:05:35,643 --> 00:05:37,875
Most writers say he cut it with his

166
00:05:37,875 --> 00:05:40,298
sword. But Aristobulus, who was there,

167
00:05:40,298 --> 00:05:42,848
says he simply pulled out the wooden pin

168
00:05:42,848 --> 00:05:43,677
that held it.

169
00:05:45,877 --> 00:05:48,941
November, three hundred thirty-three BC.

170
00:05:48,941 --> 00:05:51,315
Issus, near today's Iskenderun.

171
00:05:51,315 --> 00:05:52,771
For the first time,

172
00:05:52,771 --> 00:05:55,911
Alexander faces the Persian king himself.

173
00:05:55,911 --> 00:05:57,213
Darius the Third.

174
00:05:57,463 --> 00:05:58,571
They are so few.

175
00:05:58,571 --> 00:06:01,549
Surround them. Bring me that boy in chains.

176
00:06:01,799 --> 00:06:03,702
Companions! There is the king!

177
00:06:03,702 --> 00:06:04,526
Ride with me!

178
00:06:04,776 --> 00:06:06,919
He's going straight for Darius's chariot!

179
00:06:07,169 --> 00:06:08,874
Turn the chariot! Turn it!

180
00:06:08,874 --> 00:06:10,120
Get me out of here!

181
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:13,094
Darius ran. He left behind his treasure,

182
00:06:13,094 --> 00:06:15,033
his tent, and his own family.

183
00:06:15,433 --> 00:06:19,400
That evening, Alexander and Hephaestion visit the captured

184
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:20,973
queen mother of Persia,

185
00:06:20,973 --> 00:06:21,725
Sisygambis.

186
00:06:21,975 --> 00:06:24,688
Great king. Please. Have mercy on my family.

187
00:06:24,888 --> 00:06:26,368
My lady, I'm not the king.

188
00:06:26,368 --> 00:06:26,709
He is.

189
00:06:26,959 --> 00:06:28,416
Forgive me! I didn't know!

190
00:06:28,416 --> 00:06:29,032
Forgive me!

191
00:06:29,232 --> 00:06:30,496
You made no mistake,

192
00:06:30,496 --> 00:06:32,265
mother. He is Alexander too.

193
00:06:32,665 --> 00:06:35,362
Several ancient writers tell this story.

194
00:06:35,362 --> 00:06:38,329
And Sisygambis grew to love Alexander like a

195
00:06:38,329 --> 00:06:38,599
son.

196
00:06:40,799 --> 00:06:43,647
Next, Alexander takes the whole coast.

197
00:06:43,647 --> 00:06:45,445
The island city of Tyre,

198
00:06:45,445 --> 00:06:47,393
after a seven-month siege.

199
00:06:47,393 --> 00:06:50,391
Then Egypt, where a priest in the desert

200
00:06:50,391 --> 00:06:52,789
oasis of Siwa calls him a son of

201
00:06:52,789 --> 00:06:53,838
the god Ammon.

202
00:06:54,088 --> 00:06:54,787
Son of a god?

203
00:06:54,787 --> 00:06:56,615
Alexander, your father was Philip.

204
00:06:56,615 --> 00:06:57,207
I knew him.

205
00:06:57,407 --> 00:06:59,684
Careful, my friend. A king can have more

206
00:06:59,684 --> 00:07:00,595
than one father.

207
00:07:00,995 --> 00:07:03,872
October, three hundred thirty-one BC.

208
00:07:03,872 --> 00:07:06,672
Gaugamela, in today's northern Iraq.

209
00:07:06,672 --> 00:07:09,238
Darius has gathered a giant army,

210
00:07:09,238 --> 00:07:12,426
and chariots with blades on their wheels.

211
00:07:12,676 --> 00:07:15,722
Flatten the ground. My chariots need a smooth

212
00:07:15,722 --> 00:07:16,128
field.

213
00:07:16,428 --> 00:07:18,641
But Alexander has the phalanx.

214
00:07:18,641 --> 00:07:22,257
Sixteen men deep, holding spears called sarissas,

215
00:07:22,257 --> 00:07:23,880
up to six meters long.

216
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:25,621
Chariots! Here they come!

217
00:07:25,821 --> 00:07:27,784
Open the lines! Let them through!

218
00:07:28,034 --> 00:07:29,662
The soldiers step aside.

219
00:07:29,662 --> 00:07:31,833
The chariots race into the gaps,

220
00:07:31,833 --> 00:07:33,800
and are cut down from behind.

221
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:35,719
There! A gap in their line!

222
00:07:35,719 --> 00:07:36,919
Companions, with me!

223
00:07:37,119 --> 00:07:38,726
Darius is running again!

224
00:07:39,026 --> 00:07:41,497
Darius escaped. A few months later,

225
00:07:41,497 --> 00:07:43,333
his own nobles killed him.

226
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:45,945
Alexander was now the king of Persia.

227
00:07:48,145 --> 00:07:51,659
Persepolis. The most magnificent palace in the world,

228
00:07:51,659 --> 00:07:53,781
and now it belongs to Alexander.

229
00:07:54,031 --> 00:07:55,552
Gold! The rooms are full of gold!

230
00:07:55,752 --> 00:07:58,535
Plutarch says it took ten thousand pairs of

231
00:07:58,535 --> 00:08:01,318
mules and five thousand camels to carry the

232
00:08:01,318 --> 00:08:02,224
treasure away.

233
00:08:02,524 --> 00:08:05,103
Then, one night, after a drinking party,

234
00:08:05,103 --> 00:08:06,199
the palace burns.

235
00:08:06,449 --> 00:08:08,297
Alexander, why? It's beautiful!

236
00:08:08,497 --> 00:08:10,442
The Persians burned Athens.

237
00:08:10,442 --> 00:08:11,667
Now Persia burns.

238
00:08:11,967 --> 00:08:14,669
Was it revenge, or a drunken mistake?

239
00:08:14,669 --> 00:08:16,786
The ancient writers disagree.

240
00:08:17,086 --> 00:08:18,815
He's wearing Persian robes now.

241
00:08:18,815 --> 00:08:20,042
He has Persian guards.

242
00:08:20,042 --> 00:08:22,329
Hephaestion, does he even remember Mieza?

243
00:08:22,579 --> 00:08:24,535
He remembers. He just doesn't want to be

244
00:08:24,535 --> 00:08:25,367
that boy anymore.

245
00:08:27,567 --> 00:08:28,646
And so we're back,

246
00:08:28,646 --> 00:08:30,265
on that night in Samarkand.

247
00:08:30,565 --> 00:08:31,722
What have I done?

248
00:08:31,722 --> 00:08:32,879
Cleitus. Cleitus!

249
00:08:33,179 --> 00:08:36,270
For three days, Alexander locked himself in his

250
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:37,586
tent. He didn't eat.

251
00:08:37,586 --> 00:08:38,638
He didn't speak.

252
00:08:38,888 --> 00:08:40,215
He won't talk to anyone.

253
00:08:40,215 --> 00:08:40,879
Not even me.

254
00:08:41,129 --> 00:08:42,550
Remember what Aristotle said?

255
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:43,627
Too much is a mistake.

256
00:08:43,627 --> 00:08:44,656
He's lost the middle.

257
00:08:45,056 --> 00:08:47,785
A year later, Alexander asks his men to

258
00:08:47,785 --> 00:08:49,185
bow down before him,

259
00:08:49,185 --> 00:08:50,305
the Persian way.

260
00:08:50,555 --> 00:08:52,520
In Greece, we bow only to the gods.

261
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:53,979
I will not kneel to a man.

262
00:08:54,229 --> 00:08:56,134
You're Aristotle's own relative,

263
00:08:56,134 --> 00:08:58,337
Callisthenes. He taught you to argue.

264
00:08:58,337 --> 00:08:59,052
Not to obey.

265
00:08:59,352 --> 00:09:03,019
Callisthenes was Alexander's official historian.

266
00:09:03,019 --> 00:09:05,158
Soon after, he was arrested,

267
00:09:05,158 --> 00:09:06,839
and died in captivity.

268
00:09:07,139 --> 00:09:08,971
First Cleitus. Now Callisthenes.

269
00:09:08,971 --> 00:09:09,601
Who's next?

270
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:13,983
Three hundred twenty-six BC.

271
00:09:13,983 --> 00:09:15,776
The army reaches India,

272
00:09:15,776 --> 00:09:17,491
at the river Hydaspes.

273
00:09:17,491 --> 00:09:18,893
On the other side,

274
00:09:18,893 --> 00:09:20,608
King Porus is waiting,

275
00:09:20,608 --> 00:09:22,089
with war elephants.

276
00:09:22,339 --> 00:09:24,437
What are those? They're as big as houses!

277
00:09:24,637 --> 00:09:26,737
Elephants. The horses won't go near them.

278
00:09:27,037 --> 00:09:29,710
So Alexander crossed the river at night,

279
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:32,450
in a thunderstorm, far from Porus's camp.

280
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:34,583
Elephants forward! Crush them!

281
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:36,937
Spears up! Aim for the drivers!

282
00:09:37,187 --> 00:09:38,482
One's coming right at me!

283
00:09:38,482 --> 00:09:39,363
Shields! Shields!

284
00:09:39,663 --> 00:09:41,640
The wounded elephants panic,

285
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:43,335
and trample both armies.

286
00:09:43,335 --> 00:09:45,100
Porus fights on, wounded,

287
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:46,442
until the very end.

288
00:09:46,742 --> 00:09:48,351
How do you want to be treated?

289
00:09:48,601 --> 00:09:49,692
Like a king.

290
00:09:50,042 --> 00:09:52,897
Alexander was so impressed that he gave Porus

291
00:09:52,897 --> 00:09:55,307
his kingdom back, and more land on top

292
00:09:55,307 --> 00:09:55,688
of it.

293
00:09:55,988 --> 00:09:57,937
But soon after the battle,

294
00:09:57,937 --> 00:10:01,161
Bucephalus died, of old age and exhaustion.

295
00:10:01,161 --> 00:10:02,660
He was about thirty.

296
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:05,134
Alexander named a city after him.

297
00:10:05,534 --> 00:10:07,148
Further east! To the Ganges,

298
00:10:07,148 --> 00:10:08,588
and the end of the world.

299
00:10:08,588 --> 00:10:09,741
Just one more river.

300
00:10:09,991 --> 00:10:12,862
No, my king. Eight years we've followed you.

301
00:10:12,862 --> 00:10:14,233
Our fathers are dead.

302
00:10:14,233 --> 00:10:16,451
Our children don't know our faces.

303
00:10:16,451 --> 00:10:17,430
Let us go home.

304
00:10:17,730 --> 00:10:18,658
Then go! Go home,

305
00:10:18,658 --> 00:10:20,731
and tell them you left your king among

306
00:10:20,731 --> 00:10:21,386
his enemies!

307
00:10:21,686 --> 00:10:24,761
Once again, Alexander shut himself in his tent

308
00:10:24,761 --> 00:10:27,503
for three days. But the army didn't move.

309
00:10:27,503 --> 00:10:29,575
For the first time in his life,

310
00:10:29,575 --> 00:10:30,578
he turned back.

311
00:10:32,778 --> 00:10:35,826
The way home crosses the Gedrosian desert,

312
00:10:35,826 --> 00:10:37,931
in today's Pakistan and Iran.

313
00:10:37,931 --> 00:10:40,617
The heat kills thousands of soldiers,

314
00:10:40,617 --> 00:10:41,996
women, and animals.

315
00:10:42,246 --> 00:10:43,795
My king. We found a little water.

316
00:10:43,795 --> 00:10:44,405
It's for you.

317
00:10:44,655 --> 00:10:46,448
Drink it, Alexander. You need it.

318
00:10:46,648 --> 00:10:48,022
If my men can't drink,

319
00:10:48,022 --> 00:10:49,209
then neither can I.

320
00:10:49,559 --> 00:10:52,909
Arrian writes that Alexander poured the water into

321
00:10:52,909 --> 00:10:55,387
the sand, in front of the whole army.

322
00:10:55,387 --> 00:10:57,397
And it was as if every man had

323
00:10:57,397 --> 00:10:57,799
drunk.

324
00:10:58,199 --> 00:10:59,989
A year later, in Ecbatana,

325
00:10:59,989 --> 00:11:02,398
Hephaestion falls ill with a fever.

326
00:11:02,398 --> 00:11:03,981
Within days, he's dead.

327
00:11:04,281 --> 00:11:05,764
Alexander. He's gone.

328
00:11:06,014 --> 00:11:08,008
Then half of me is gone with him.

329
00:11:10,208 --> 00:11:13,489
Babylon. June, three hundred twenty-three BC.

330
00:11:13,489 --> 00:11:15,750
After a long night of drinking,

331
00:11:15,750 --> 00:11:17,427
Alexander gets a fever.

332
00:11:17,427 --> 00:11:18,812
It doesn't go away.

333
00:11:19,062 --> 00:11:20,523
Alexander. Can you hear me?

334
00:11:20,523 --> 00:11:20,956
It's me.

335
00:11:21,206 --> 00:11:22,955
Remember Mieza? The shade.

336
00:11:22,955 --> 00:11:25,780
The caves. Aristotle and his long lessons.

337
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:27,591
I remember everything.

338
00:11:27,841 --> 00:11:29,346
I wanted to be the greatest.

339
00:11:29,346 --> 00:11:31,280
Maybe I was. But I never learned the

340
00:11:31,280 --> 00:11:31,656
middle.

341
00:11:32,056 --> 00:11:33,487
My king. Who gets the empire?

342
00:11:33,687 --> 00:11:34,960
To the strongest.

343
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:37,076
Did he really say that?

344
00:11:37,076 --> 00:11:39,119
Some ancient writers report it.

345
00:11:39,119 --> 00:11:41,624
Others say he could no longer speak at

346
00:11:41,624 --> 00:11:41,888
all.

347
00:11:42,188 --> 00:11:43,852
Alexander died in June,

348
00:11:43,852 --> 00:11:46,023
three hundred twenty-three BC.

349
00:11:46,023 --> 00:11:49,424
He was thirty-two. His generals fought over his

350
00:11:49,424 --> 00:11:51,740
empire for the next forty years.

351
00:11:53,940 --> 00:11:56,078
So, how much of this really happened?

352
00:11:56,328 --> 00:11:57,926
Here's the strange part.

353
00:11:57,926 --> 00:12:01,188
Every full history of Alexander that survives was

354
00:12:01,188 --> 00:12:04,185
written three to four hundred years after his

355
00:12:04,185 --> 00:12:04,584
death.

356
00:12:04,834 --> 00:12:05,871
But people were there!

357
00:12:05,871 --> 00:12:07,191
Didn't anyone write it down?

358
00:12:07,391 --> 00:12:09,596
They did. Ptolemy, Aristobulus,

359
00:12:09,596 --> 00:12:12,654
Callisthenes. But all their books are lost.

360
00:12:12,654 --> 00:12:14,645
We only have pieces of them,

361
00:12:14,645 --> 00:12:16,352
quoted by later writers.

362
00:12:16,652 --> 00:12:18,991
And one famous line is a myth: that

363
00:12:18,991 --> 00:12:22,266
Alexander cried because there were no more worlds

364
00:12:22,266 --> 00:12:25,541
to conquer. Plutarch actually wrote the opposite.

365
00:12:25,541 --> 00:12:28,415
He cried because there were endless worlds,

366
00:12:28,415 --> 00:12:30,620
and he hadn't even conquered one.

367
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:33,592
And his tomb? It stood in Alexandria,

368
00:12:33,592 --> 00:12:36,192
in Egypt. Roman emperors visited it.

369
00:12:36,192 --> 00:12:39,080
Then it vanished. Nobody has found it to

370
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:39,730
this day.

371
00:12:40,030 --> 00:12:42,253
Twenty years. From a stone bench in Mieza,

372
00:12:42,253 --> 00:12:43,365
to the edge of India.

373
00:12:43,365 --> 00:12:44,953
And I was there for all of it.

374
00:12:45,303 --> 00:12:47,372
So, if you'd been in that tent in

375
00:12:47,372 --> 00:12:49,692
Samarkand, would you have kept quiet?

376
00:12:49,692 --> 00:12:51,071
Or told him the truth,

377
00:12:51,071 --> 00:12:53,454
like Cleitus? Tell me in the comments.
